    "innovation": "This paper introduces a conceptual framework that assesses adaptation options to sudden ecosystem crashes by considering the portability of the ecosystem service and the locational flexibility of the stakeholder.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "drought that occurred around 2000",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "US southwest",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "One of the most extensively documented examples of a sudden ecosystem crash in response to climate change is the drought-induced tree die-off in pi\u00f1on-juniper woodlands in the US southwest accompanying a drought that occurred around 2000 (Breshears et al. 2005, 2009; Shaw et al. 2005).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "2000 drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "New Mexico",
            "target_entity_type": "Country_Subdivision",
            "paragraph": "During the 2000 drought, many homeowners were impacted by the die-off when tree deaths reduced privacy from nearby homes and removal of dead tree fire hazards raised expenses. Loss of the symbolic trees around their homes has alarmed many New Mexicans.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "2000 drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Santa Fe",
            "target_entity_type": "City",
            "paragraph": "During the 2000 drought, many homeowners were impacted by the die-off when tree deaths reduced privacy from nearby homes and removal of dead tree fire hazards raised expenses. Loss of the symbolic trees around their homes has alarmed many New Mexicans. An urban wildlife specialist for the city of Santa Fe said that during the die-off she ''had people crying on the phone'' over the loss of their pi\u00f1on trees (Carlton 2006).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
